29,2,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
30,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings show a worrying trend. The late-turn pool is severely thinned, and we are relying heavily on overtime for final departures and overnight works. Our roadway and signals crews are also significantly understaffed.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,Takumi,,The pilots from eight years ago actually worked! The contactless ferry trial and the late-evening timetable were both successes. Why did we stop? We keep ignoring the things that actually worked in the past.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The joint operators' committee minutes reveal a lack of agreement on passenger data between ferry and rail. Furthermore, work inside pre-war shells is now heavily priced due to frequent, unrecorded voids causing major delays.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I found that the ticketing contractor fees are now higher than our resurfacing budget. Also, the technology and operating subsidies were trimmed, though there is some ring-fenced construction money left from a lapsed grant.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Sakura,,"I found a mystery! The mid-city crowds are clearing just fine, but the trips are taking way longer than they used to. Also, people aren't waiting at machines; they're waiting for connections! The time is hiding in the gaps!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Takumi, it is encouraging that the pilots worked, but we must ask if we have the staff to restart them. If the late-turn pool is this thin, adding more services could break the remaining crews.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, we can't just let the staff shortage stop us from doing what works. If the late-evening pilot met its cost targets, maybe that money can be used to fix the staffing problem!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, funding is one thing, but the physical reality is another. If we choose a plan involving pre-war shells, no amount of budget will prevent the delays caused by unrecorded voids.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I agree with Toshio. And I'm worried that if we just move money around, we might ignore the fact that our technology and operating subsidies are already being trimmed. We have to be careful.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Sakura,,"@Daisuke, don't be so worried! But @Victoria, if the time is hiding in the connections, maybe we don't need more trains, just better timing? That way the tired crews can still get their naps!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Daisuke"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Sakura, better timing is a rational approach, but even a schedule shift requires oversight. My concern is that any plan requiring increased maintenance or late-night shifts will fail without more personnel.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Victoria, you're too cautious! We can't just freeze in place because we're understaffed. We need to pick a plan that actually delivers results, like those pilots did, and then fight to get the resources we need.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, fighting for resources after a project stalls is a costly strategy. We should prioritize a plan that avoids the bureaucratic friction of the joint operators and the physical risks of the old shells.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think Toshio is right. If we pick something too complex, we might just end up arguing about where the money comes from while the project fails. Maybe we should look for something... simpler?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Sakura,,"Simpler is better! If we just fix the gaps where people are waiting, it's like a puzzle where the pieces finally fit. We don't need to dig holes in old shells or make everyone work overtime!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I agree with Sakura. A plan that optimizes existing connections rather than expanding service or digging into old infrastructure is the only way to avoid overloading our depleted crews.,"{""words"":29,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm not just going to settle for ""simple"" if it means ignoring what we know works. If the contactless trial was a success, we should be using that to fix the gaps Sakura found!","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Sakura""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"@Takumi, the contactless trial was a success, but if it requires new data agreements between ferry and rail, it will stall in committee. We must avoid any plan that depends on those unresolved disputes.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think we should avoid anything that relies on those disputes. If the budget is already being trimmed, we can't afford a project that gets stuck in arguments. A simpler, more stable plan seems safer.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Sakura,,Yay! Everyone is starting to agree with me! Let's find a plan that's like a magic trick—fixing the waiting times without making the workers tired or the bosses grumpy. I'm ready to see the options!,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,3,,status,Daisuke,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":1,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
